# Windows batch files must keep CRLF line endings. cmd.exe resolves `goto :label` by
# scanning the file, and with bare LF endings it can miss labels, so a script that works
# on the author's machine aborts on someone else's. This pins the endings no matter what
# core.autocrlf says on the machine that checks the files out (or a ZIP download).
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# And the opposite for shell scripts: a CRLF shebang line stops them from running at all.
*.sh text eol=lf
backend/gradlew text eol=lf
